\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The 15 classes of ordered unit pairs}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{For the \vocabk{one-vertex Hurwitz model}{fdip-00ZA},
let #{c_{s,t}} count the ordered pairs with scalars
#{s,t} as in \ref{fdip-00ZD}. Its nonzero values are
##{\begin{array}{r|r|r}
s&t&c_{s,t}\\\hline
0&0&1\\
1&0&16\\
2&0&28\\
2&2&48\\
3&0&16\\
3&2&96\\
4&0&46\\
4&2&96\\
4&4&24\\
5&0&16\\
5&2&96\\
6&0&28\\
6&2&48\\
7&0&16\\
8&0&1
\end{array}}
The counts sum to #{576}.}

\proof{
\p{The explicit list in \ref{fdip-00V1} splits into
#{1}, #{-1}, the 6 axial units
#{\pm\qi,\pm\qj,\pm\qk}, and two sets of #{8}
half-integer units with real part #{1/2} or #{-1/2}.
By \ref{fdip-00ZI}, their individual values of
#{N(U^{-1}-1)} are respectively #{0,4,2,1,3}.}

\p{A central factor makes #{t=0}. The 4 ordered
central pairs contribute #{1,2,1} at #{s=0,4,8}.
Pairs with one central and one axial factor contribute
#{12} at each of #{s=2,6}. Pairs with one central and
one half-integer factor contribute #{16} at each of
#{s=1,3,5,7}. These counts include both orders.}

\p{Among the #{36} axial pairs, each first unit has two
second units on its own axis. The resulting #{12} pairs
have #{s=4,t=0}; the other #{24} pairs have #{s=4,t=4}.
For an axial and a half-integer unit the imaginary squared
lengths are #{1,3/4}, with squared dot product #{1/4}.
Thus #{t=2}. Each real-part sign of the half-integer unit
gives #{2\cdot6\cdot8=96} pairs in both orders, at
#{s=3} and #{s=5}, respectively.}

\p{Fix the ordered real-part signs of two half-integer
units. There are #{8\cdot8=64} imaginary sign pairs.
For each first sign triple, two second triples are equal
or opposite to it. These #{16} choices have squared dot
product #{9/16} and #{t=0}. The remaining #{48} have
squared dot product #{1/16} and #{t=2}, since both
squared lengths are #{3/4}. Two positive real parts give
#{s=2}, two negative real parts give #{s=6}, and the two
mixed sign orders give #{s=4}. Adding these disjoint
cases gives the table; in particular the count at
#{(4,0)} is #{2+12+32=46}. Their total is
#{4+24+64+36+192+256=576}.}
}
